## Levels contrast (NOT effect estimates: spurious trend)
LMC log GNIpc ~ edu + trend: edu -0.2002 (SE 0.0658, t=-3.04), R2=0.919, **DW=0.52**.
SSA same spec (n=5): edu -0.0662 (SE 0.0315, t=-2.10).
Levels are dominated by common trends; differenced estimates are the credible ones.

## Inference quality (why the LMC positive slope is weak)
- Leave-one-year-out, LMC A1: b ranges [-0.0049, +0.0293]. Dropping **1976 alone** moves A1 from +0.0219 to -0.0049 and A2 from +0.0151 to +0.0017.
- Stationary block bootstrap (mean block 5): A1 95% CI [-0.034,+0.039] boot p=0.33; **A2 CI [-0.0096,+0.0332] boot p=0.16** (P(b>0)=0.92); A3 CI [+0.003,+0.052] boot p=0.028.
- Subperiods: 1976-90 b=+0.0368 (t=4.37, n=15) vs 1991-2015 b=-0.0312 (t=-0.70, n=25). The positive sign is a pre-1991 phenomenon.
- SSA naive bootstrap CI on A1: [-0.88,+0.09] — uninformative at n=4.

## Robustness on the primary LMC slope
winsor export growth +-20pp: +0.0157 (t=2.99); HAC lags 1/8: SE 0.0150/0.0103; symmetric 2011-15 window: -0.1194 (t=-5.83, n=5);
drop SSA growth year 2012: LMC unchanged (+0.0151), SSA becomes unidentifiable (n=3).
Raw export growth retained in the headline per instruction.

## Formal group comparison — power-limited, not equivalence
Interaction dy~d_edu*SSA: -0.0735 under NW(4) (SE 0.0172, t=-4.28, p<.001) BUT +0.0735-magnitude coefficient has
classical SE 0.1418 (t=-0.52). The NW "significance" is an artifact of estimating HAC weights from 4 SSA rows; do not report it as significant.
Chow F(2,40)=0.134, p=0.875 (n=44, only 4 SSA rows; SSR_r=0.02339 vs SSR_ur=0.02323).
Simple slope gap SSA-LMC = -0.0735 (SE 0.0258, t=-2.85) but SEs are not comparable across n=4 vs n=40.

## Mechanism / descriptive contrast (supports the comparison)
Education-investment share, SSA minus LMC: +0.80 pp (1975-2015), +0.77 pp (1991-2015), +0.25 pp (2011-15).
2015 levels: GNIpc SSA $1,634 vs LMC $1,965; mean growth SSA 1.24%/yr vs LMC 2.01%/yr;
secondary GER SSA 43.4 vs LMC 64.5; primary GER SSA 97.0 vs LMC 101.8.
Reduced form d(GER)~d_edu: LMC primary -0.781 (t=-1.71), secondary -0.786 (t=-2.71); SSA +0.249 (t=0.54), -0.264 (t=-1.25).
Year-to-year spending shifts do not translate into enrollment gains in either group.

## Limitations
Reverse causality and a mechanical share (denominator = outcome GNI); no instruments or country fixed effects
possible at N=2; SSA outcome support of 4 changes; group membership overlap between SSA and LMC;
export-growth outliers; GER>100 in LMC; single-year leverage (1976).
